  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Cold front passing through this evening bringing gusty winds to\n   western Montana and north central Idaho\n\n-  Rain and high elevation snow showers across north central \n   Idaho and northwest Montana tonight into Saturday morning\n\nA cold front will continue to move through western Montana this\nafternoon, with the main impact being gusty winds. High resolution\nmodels continue to show the strongest winds through the Mission\nand Bitterroot valleys and along the Highway 2 corridor from Kila\nto Happy's Inn, where local wind gusts could approach 45 mph, \nmost likely between 2pm and 8pm tonight. Wind will also cause\nchoppy, hazardous conditions on area lakes, including over \nFlathead Lake into this evening. Wind advisories remain in effect.\n\nSecondary to the wind will be the development of showers and\npossibly an isolated thunderstorm with and behind the frontal\npassage. North central Idaho and northwest Montana have the best\nchances of seeing measurable precipitation. Snow levels will drop\nwith the front to around 5000 feet by Saturday morning. This will\nlikely bring a light dusting of snow to the higher elevations of\nnorthwest Montana, including in Glacier National Park. The\nmoisture feed with this system is minimal however with\nprecipitation ending pretty quickly behind frontal passage. Those\nin the backcountry should be prepared for a change to cool,\nshowery and raw conditions with a threat for hypothermia into \nSaturday morning.\n\nThe majority of forecast models show a zonal (westerly flow\npattern) over the region into early next week. This will mainly\nlead to drier but breezy conditions with temperatures near normal\nfor this time of year.\n\nForecast models continue to show lower confidence in the pattern\ngoing into next weekend with regard to a potential low pressure\nsystem. Right now, around 30 percent of model solutions show a\nmuch colder and deeper trough, with the remaining 70 percent\neither showing continued zonal flow or a weaker trough passage.",
